{"term":"grzesznik cielesny","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"fornicator","concept":"fornicator","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":15,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":72,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A carnal sinner; a person who has committed sins of the flesh in the Catholic moral framework, covering any mortal sexual transgression including fornication, adultery, or masturbation.","english_term":"carnal sinner","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Broader than the English 'fornicator,' which implies only pre-marital sex; grzesznik cielesny covers all sexual mortal sins in Catholic theology. Recognition declining with falling church attendance among youth."}
{"term":"niemoralne zachowanie","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"indecency","concept":"indecency","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":10,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":92,"recognition_pct_peak":92,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Immoral behavior; conduct that violates moral or religious standards of decency, most commonly invoked in sexual contexts by clergy, conservative media, and Catholic public discourse.","english_term":"immoral behavior","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Generic phrase — context determines whether immorality is sexual or otherwise. Very high recognition as an everyday compound. Used both in religious homiletics and secular moral condemnation. Not inherently sexual without context."}
{"term":"niemoralność","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"licentiousness","concept":"licentiousness","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":10,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Immorality; the state or quality of being morally unrestrained, especially in sexual matters, as judged by religious or traditional Catholic standards.","english_term":"immorality","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Abstract noun form. In Polish Catholic public discourse predominantly refers to sexual immorality. Carries moral condemnation but no intrinsic vulgarity — found in broadsheet journalism, Church documents, and political rhetoric equally."}
{"term":"grzech nieczystości","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"fornication","concept":"fornication","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":15,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":68,"recognition_pct_peak":82,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Sin of impurity; the Catholic sacramental umbrella term for all mortal sins of a sexual nature, including fornication, masturbation, adultery, and sodomy, as used in confession and catechism.","english_term":"sin of impurity","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Specifically confessional-Catholic vocabulary; penitents use it as a catch-all when confessing sexual sins without naming the specific act. Broader than English 'fornication' which covers only pre-marital intercourse. Recognition declining with declining sacramental practice."}
{"term":"cielesny grzech","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"fornication","concept":"fornication","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":15,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":70,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Carnal sin; sin of the flesh; the Catholic moral label for any sexual transgression, foregrounding the bodily/fleshly nature of the sin against spiritual purity.","english_term":"carnal sin","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Synonymous with grzech nieczystości but carries a slightly more literary and theological flavor through the adjective 'cielesny' (bodily/carnal). Word-order variant 'grzech cielesny' also exists. Evokes the Pauline body-spirit dualism central to Catholic sexual ethics."}
{"term":"brud moralny","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"debauchery","concept":"debauchery","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":20,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":62,"recognition_pct_peak":75,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Moral filth; a rhetorical phrase in Polish Catholic and conservative discourse for sexual immorality, framing it as spiritual and moral contamination through the pollution metaphor.","english_term":"moral filth","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Strongly metaphorical — uses brud (dirt, filth, grime) as a purity metaphor for spiritual corruption. Common in sermons targeting pornography, homosexuality, or premarital sex. Not a clinical or legal term. Rhetorical rather than descriptive register."}
{"term":"grzeszne pragnienia","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"concupiscence","concept":"concupiscence","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":15,"technical":25,"recognition_pct_current":70,"recognition_pct_peak":78,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Sinful desires; the yearning for carnal or sexual pleasure identified in Catholic theology as the inherited inclination toward sin, equivalent to the theological concept of concupiscence in pastoral Polish language.","english_term":"sinful desires","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Near-direct pastoral equivalent of theological concupiscence. The formal theological term is 'pożądliwość'; 'grzeszne pragnienia' is the more accessible homiletic version used in sermons and catechism. Direct conceptual match in the Catholic moral framework."}
{"term":"życie w grzechu","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"cohabitation","concept":"cohabitation","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":10,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":87,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Living in sin; cohabitation of a sexually active unmarried couple, framed through Catholic moral condemnation of domestic arrangements outside the sacrament of marriage.","english_term":"living in sin","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Direct structural and semantic calque of the English phrase 'living in sin.' Both phrases carry identical moralizing Catholic register. Used by clergy, devout Catholics, and older generations to describe unmarried cohabitation. Younger secular Poles use it neutrally or ironically."}
{"term":"konkubinat","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"cohabitation","concept":"cohabitation","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":10,"technical":35,"recognition_pct_current":72,"recognition_pct_peak":78,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Concubinage; the formal legal and ecclesiastical term for cohabitation as an unmarried sexual couple, used in Church documents, Polish civil law discussions, and conservative media.","english_term":"concubinage","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Loanword from Latin concubinatus. More formal and legalistic than życie w grzechu; carries overtones of the historical institution of the concubine rather than just modern cohabitation. In modern secular Polish usage increasingly neutral, simply denoting an unofficial domestic partnership."}
{"term":"cudzołóstwo","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"adultery","concept":"adultery","is_novel_concept":true,"register":"moral_religious","crude":20,"technical":30,"recognition_pct_current":92,"recognition_pct_peak":94,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Adultery; sexual intercourse in which at least one partner is married to someone else, constituting a major mortal sin in Catholic doctrine and historically a legal offense under Polish law.","english_term":"adultery","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Canonical Polish term enshrined in the Sixth Commandment ('Nie cudzołóż'). Distinct from fornication (sex between unmarried people) — cudzołóstwo specifically requires at least one married party. Extremely high recognition due to biblical ubiquity. Proposed novel concept slug 'adultery' as the registry lacks this distinct concept."}
{"term":"cudzołożnik","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"adultery","concept":"adultery","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":20,"technical":25,"recognition_pct_current":82,"recognition_pct_peak":86,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Adulterer; a man who commits adultery, i.e., who engages in sexual relations outside his marriage or with a married person.","english_term":"adulterer","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Masculine agent noun derived from cudzołóstwo. Feminine equivalent is cudzołożnica. Found in biblical translations, Catholic homiletics, and historical legal texts. Carries strong moral condemnation in Catholic Poland. References the novel concept 'adultery' introduced in the preceding record."}
{"term":"zhańbienie","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"sexual_dishonoring","concept":"sexual_dishonoring","is_novel_concept":true,"register":"moral_religious","crude":20,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":68,"recognition_pct_peak":82,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Sexual dishonoring; the act or state of bringing irreversible shame and social disgrace upon a person — typically a woman — through a sexual transgression such as premarital sex, rape, or adultery, within a patriarchal honor economy.","english_term":"sexual dishonoring","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"English 'slut-shaming' captures the social shaming mechanism but not the gravity or permanence; zhańbienie implies a lasting stain on honor with real social consequences (reduced marriage prospects, family ostracism). 'Ravishment' in its archaic sense covers only the assault dimension. Culturally specific to Catholic Polish honor culture — proposed novel concept 'sexual_dishonoring.'"}
{"term":"hańba rodziny","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"family_sexual_shame","concept":"family_sexual_shame","is_novel_concept":true,"register":"moral_religious","crude":20,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Family shame; the collective dishonor brought upon an entire family unit by a member's — typically a daughter's — sexual transgression, including premarital pregnancy, scandal, or assault, within the Polish Catholic honor economy.","english_term":"family shame","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The shame is explicitly collective: the entire family, not just the individual, is dishonored and loses social standing in the community. No English equivalent encapsulates this collective honor-economy concept with its specific sexual valence. Still invoked in traditionalist rural and conservative Catholic communities. Proposed novel concept 'family_sexual_shame.'"}
{"term":"zhańbić się","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"sexual_dishonoring","concept":"slut_shaming","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":20,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":63,"recognition_pct_peak":78,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"To bring dishonor upon oneself; the reflexive act of forfeiting one's sexual honor through a transgression that causes shame to oneself and one's family in a patriarchal honor economy.","english_term":"to dishonor oneself sexually","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Reflexive perfective verb form of zhańbić (to dishonor). Approximate match to slut_shaming: the honor-shame mechanism is the same but the agency is self-directed rather than externally imposed. A woman 'zhańbi się' by her own act; slut_shaming describes others' reaction. Less recognized than the noun zhańbienie."}
{"term":"utrata honoru","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"sexual_honor_loss","concept":"sexual_honor_loss","is_novel_concept":true,"register":"moral_religious","crude":10,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":78,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Loss of honor; in traditional Polish Catholic culture, the irreversible forfeiture of a woman's social and moral standing resulting from any sexual act that violates the community's honor code — premarital sex, assault, adultery, or public scandal.","english_term":"loss of sexual honor","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Broader than 'defloration' (which targets only virginity loss) and broader than 'slut-shaming' (which targets social practice). Historically had concrete economic consequences: reduced bride price, broken engagements, family ostracism. Still invoked in conservative Catholic and rural communities. Proposed novel concept 'sexual_honor_loss.'"}
{"term":"wstyd seksualny","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"sexual_shame","concept":"sexual_repression","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":10,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":75,"recognition_pct_peak":75,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Sexual shame; the internalized feeling of guilt, embarrassment, or humiliation regarding one's own sexual desires, behavior, or body, typically rooted in Catholic upbringing or patriarchal norms.","english_term":"sexual shame","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"More of a psychological/emotional state than a moral label. Increasingly common in contemporary Polish sex therapy discourse and feminist writing. Approximate match to sexual_repression — wstyd seksualny describes the felt shame itself; repression describes the behavioral suppression that typically results from it. The concepts are related but distinct."}
{"term":"puszczalska","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"slut_shame","concept":"slut_shame","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":78,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"An 'easy' or sexually permissive woman; a derogatory slur for a woman perceived as willing to have sex indiscriminately or without selectivity or emotional commitment.","english_term":"slut","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Derived from the verb puścić się (to let oneself go, to be easy sexually). Less raw in register than zdzira or dziwka — implies looseness and lack of standards rather than prostitution per se. One of the most common everyday Polish insults directed at women. Approximate rather than direct because 'slut' carries heavier connotations of pure vulgarity; puszczalska is more about the concept of being 'easy.'"}
{"term":"szmata","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"slut_shame","concept":"slut_shame","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":80,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":92,"recognition_pct_peak":92,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Rag; when applied to a woman, a derogatory slur comparing her to a filthy, used-up piece of cloth to denote sexual promiscuity and low social worth.","english_term":"slag","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Literal meaning is dishcloth or rag — the sexual insult operates through the pollution/used-up metaphor. Also used as a gender-neutral insult meaning spineless pushover (for men: 'he's a szmata') making the sexual sense context-dependent. Closest English equivalent is British 'slag' which similarly implies cheapness/used-up quality."}
{"term":"zdzira","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"slut_shame","concept":"slut_shame","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":93,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":93,"recognition_pct_peak":93,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"One of the most offensive Polish slurs for a sexually promiscuous woman; broadly equivalent in force and deployment to English 'slut' or 'whore,' used in heated arguments, street insults, and as an intensified misogynistic attack.","english_term":"slut","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Among the harshest single-word insults in Polish when directed at a woman. Etymology unclear — possibly related to zdzierać (to scrape, wear out), reinforcing the 'used up' metaphor. Ubiquitous across generations and social classes. Public use against a named individual is prosecutable under Polish civil law as an insult (zniesławienie)."}
{"term":"lafirynda","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"promiscuous_woman_slur","concept":"promiscuous_woman_slur","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"dated_slang","crude":55,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":68,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":1975,"regional":null,"definition":"A floozy; a dated derogatory term for a sexually loose or promiscuous woman with a somewhat theatrical, grandmotherly contempt — implying moral looseness, gaudy appearance, and low social standing.","english_term":"floozy","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Archaic in active use — primarily vocabulary of older generations and period drama. Carries a slightly comic or theatrical tone that has blunted its offensiveness; more likely to provoke amusement than outrage from younger speakers. Possibly derived from German or Yiddish. Recognition declining with generational turnover."}
{"term":"flądra","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"slut_shame","concept":"slut_shame","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":68,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":82,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Flounder (the fish); when applied to a woman, a crude slur denoting sexual promiscuity — the flat-lying fish evokes a woman supine and sexually available to anyone.","english_term":"slut","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Literal meaning is the flatfish species (Platichthys flesus). The sexual insult is zoomorphic — the fish's flat prone posture evokes a woman lying on her back. Also used regionally for a lazy or slovenly woman regardless of sexual connotation. Less severe than zdzira; the zoological origin lends it a slightly cartoonish quality."}
{"term":"dziwka","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"slut_shame","concept":"slut_shame","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":95,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":97,"recognition_pct_peak":97,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Whore; the most ubiquitous and offensive Polish slur for a woman, used both literally for sex workers and as a maximally intensified general insult against any woman whose behavior the speaker condemns.","english_term":"whore","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Etymology uncertain — possibly from dziwna (strange creature) or dialectal dziwa. Saturation-level recognition and use across all generations, social classes, and regions; appears in football stadium chants, political invective, domestic arguments, and cinema. Public directed use against a named person is civilly actionable. The single most socially loaded word in Polish directed at women."}
{"term":"ladacznica","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"sex_worker_archaic","concept":"sex_worker_archaic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":38,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":63,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":1960,"regional":null,"definition":"Harlot; an archaic Polish term for a sexually loose woman or prostitute, primarily preserved in biblical translations, 19th-century literature, and historical texts.","english_term":"harlot","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Appears in the Polish Bible (e.g., Rahab the ladacznica in Joshua). Has effectively zero offensive charge in modern colloquial speech due to total archaism — reads as literary or historical rather than insulting. High recognition among educated speakers and churchgoers; low among youth. Etymological origin disputed — possibly from lada (shelf, trifle) or a Slavic root for 'slattern.'"}
{"term":"wywłoka","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"slut_shame","concept":"slut_shame","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":75,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":72,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A crude slur for a sexually promiscuous or loose woman; also used more broadly for a contemptible, slovenly, or morally degraded person of either sex.","english_term":"slut","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Possibly related to wywłóczyć (to drag out, to haul around), with the implication of a woman who 'gets dragged around' between men. Less universally recognized than zdzira or dziwka; more regional variation in usage. When applied to men it functions as general contempt without sexual connotation."}
{"term":"rozwiązła","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"licentiousness","concept":"licentiousness","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":40,"technical":30,"recognition_pct_current":72,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Dissolute woman; the feminine form of the adjective rozwiązły, denoting a woman who is sexually or morally unrestrained, licentious, and heedless of sexual propriety.","english_term":"licentious","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Feminine adjectival form of rozwiązły (dissolute, licentious); functions as both adjective ('kobieta rozwiązła' = a dissolute woman) and substantivized noun ('ta rozwiązła' = that loose woman). More literary and elevated in register than zdzira or puszczalska; found in journalism, moral essays, and literary fiction. Lower crude score reflects the formal register — the word shames through elevated language rather than street invective."}
